  "worked_solution": "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f$ is $\\pi $-periodic, shifting by $2\\pi $ does not change the value of $f,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f((x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\pi )=f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does not always equal $f(x).$ For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=F(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})\\ne f(x)$ for $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
